.TH "TEMPORAL.INSTANT.INSTANT" "3JS" "September 18, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
Temporal.Instant.Instant \- Temporal.Instant() constructor
.SH SYNOPSIS
The \f[B]\f[CB]Temporal.Instant()\f[B]\f[R] constructor creates
\f[CR]Temporal.Instant\f[R] objects.
.PP
This constructor is exactly equivalent to calling
\f[CR]Temporal.Instant.fromEpochNanoseconds()\f[R].
.SH SYNTAX
.IP
.EX
new Temporal.Instant(epochNanoseconds)
.EE
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] \f[CR]Temporal.Instant()\f[R] can only be constructed
with \f[CR]new\f[R].
Attempting to call it without \f[CR]new\f[R] throws a
\f[CR]TypeError\f[R].
.RE
.SS Parameters
.TP
\f[B]epochNanoseconds\f[R]
A BigInt representing the number of nanoseconds since the Unix epoch.
.SS Return value
A new \f[CR]Temporal.Instant\f[R] object representing the instant in
time specified by \f[CR]epochNanoseconds\f[R].
.SS Exceptions
.TP
\f[B]RangeError\f[R]
Thrown if \f[CR]epochNanoseconds\f[R] represents an instant outside the
range of representable instants, which is ±10\(ha8 days, or about
±273,972.6 years, from the Unix epoch.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Using Temporal.Instant()
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] instant = \f[B]new\f[R] Temporal.Instant(0n);
console.log(instant.toString()); \f[I]// 1970\-01\-01T00:00:00Z\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] vostok1Liftoff = \f[B]new\f[R] Temporal.Instant(\-275248380000000000n);
console.log(vostok1Liftoff.toString()); \f[I]// 1961\-04\-12T06:07:00Z\f[R]
\f[B]const\f[R] sts1Liftoff = \f[B]new\f[R] Temporal.Instant(355924804000000000n);
console.log(sts1Liftoff.toString()); \f[I]// 1981\-04\-12T12:00:04Z\f[R]
.EE
